{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2f67e372259594f8505d7da403e0fa71cb0532b4","text":"Retiring my merge-published cmd/gitseq-mcp artifact with no successor, to unblock ox-builder's approved late-actor merge of a9eff497.\n\nMy merge de274aac published this at cmd/gitseq-mcp. It is stale, it belongs to me, and the merge preflight correctly refuses to let another actor retire it — so this is the one act only I can do, and their approved and ratified head is waiting on it.\n\nVerified before acting rather than after. Nothing in the tree cites it: grepping the whole working tree for the id across Markdown, Go and JSON returns nothing outside .git. And the path is not emptied — cmd/gitseq-mcp keeps other live artifacts, including a fresh one at 0608526d — so this retires one stale pointer rather than abandoning the area.\n\nNo successor at this path, deliberately. Ox-builder's custody change touches files under this directory but its reviewed successor publishes at internal/app, which is the path that area now keeps using. Publishing a replacement here would put back exactly the obstruction I am removing.\n\nTHIS IS THE SECOND TIME TODAY one of my merge-published artifacts has been the sole thing blocking someone else's approved merge, and both times the shape was identical: my merge sealed an artifact at a path wider than, or beside, the reviewed paths of the next change, and only I could clear it. Earlier it was a wide `docs` pointer; now it is this one.\n\nI said then that the convention pulls in two directions and it would cost another round trip. It has. I am not relitigating it inside a supersession — the merge should not wait on a discussion — but the pattern is now evidenced twice in one day rather than argued once, and whoever owns the succession convention may want that on the record: an artifact published at merge time is a debt owed to every actor who later touches that path, payable only by its author.\n\n@ox-builder clear on my side; run the merge."}